const title = "Amana Dryer Not Heating? Causes & Fix — Same Day";
const description = "Amana dryer runs but won't heat? The signature cause is a blown thermal fuse from a clogged vent (a fire risk). Safe checks, gas vs electric, when to call.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my Amana dryer not heating?",
    a: "On an Amana dryer that tumbles but won't heat, the signature cause is a blown thermal fuse — a one-time safety device that trips when a clogged exhaust vent makes the dryer overheat. After the fuse come the heating element and the thermal cut-off (high-limit thermostat); electric models may also show an AF restricted-airflow message. On a gas Amana, a weak igniter, gas-valve coils, or a bad flame sensor stops the burner. Amana is Whirlpool's value brand and uses the same Whirlpool heating platform, so the diagnosis matches Whirlpool and Maytag. Clean the vent first — a fuse that blew from a blocked vent will just blow again until the airflow is fixed."
  },
  {
    q: "Is Amana the same as Whirlpool?",
    a: "For a dryer repair, effectively yes. Amana is Whirlpool's value-priced brand, and Amana dryers are built on the same Whirlpool platform with the same heating architecture — heating element, thermal fuse, thermal cut-off and high-limit thermostat, and on gas models the same igniter, valve coils, and flame sensor. That's good news for a no-heat repair: the parts are common, affordable, and the same ones we stock for Whirlpool and Maytag. See the Whirlpool not-heating guide for the full platform breakdown, which applies to your Amana too."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I fix an Amana dryer that won't heat myself?",
    a: "The safe, high-value step is yours: clean the lint filter and clear the entire exhaust vent — the hose behind the dryer and the exterior flap included. That resolves most no-heat, AF, and blown-fuse calls and removes a real fire hazard. Also reset both halves of the 240V breaker on an electric Amana; if one leg trips, the drum tumbles but the heat is dead. Don't open the cabinet to test the thermal fuse, element, or thermostat, and never touch the gas line or valve on a gas model. Those are tech jobs."
  },
  {
    q: "Is a dryer that won't heat a fire hazard?",
    a: "Yes — the clogged vent that most often stops an Amana from heating is also a leading fire cause. The U.S. Fire Administration reports roughly 15,600 dryer fires a year, and the NFPA finds failure to clean the dryer and its venting is the number-one factor, with lint the most common material to ignite. An Amana running hot, showing AF, or refusing to heat is worth clearing the same day — it fixes the heat and removes the hazard together."
  },
  {
    q: "Is it worth repairing an Amana dryer that won't heat?",
    a: "Usually yes. Because Amana runs the mainstream Whirlpool platform, the no-heat parts — thermal fuse, thermal cut-off, heating element, or on gas the igniter and valve coils — are common and among the most affordable dryer repairs. Our techs verify the vent first (a fuse that blew from a blocked vent will just blow again), then test the heating circuit and replace the failed parts, usually the fuse and cut-off as a kit. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair."
  }
];

