/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">An Amana that won't heat almost always blew a thermal fuse.</h2>

    <p><strong>When an Amana dryer tumbles but won't heat, the signature cause is a blown thermal fuse — a one-time safety device that trips when a clogged exhaust vent makes the dryer overheat. After the fuse come the heating element and the thermal cut-off; electric models may show an AF restricted-airflow message. On a gas Amana, it's a weak igniter, gas-valve coils, or the flame sensor. Amana is Whirlpool's value brand and runs the same Whirlpool heating platform, so the diagnosis matches Whirlpool and Maytag — and the parts are common and affordable. Clean the filter and full vent first; a fuse that blew from a blocked vent will just blow again until the airflow is fixed. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Why the vent comes first — and fast:</strong> the lint-clogged vent that stops your Amana from heating is a leading cause of house fires. The U.S. Fire Administration counts about 15,600 dryer fires a year, and the NFPA finds failure to clean the dryer and its venting is the number-one factor. A dryer running hot, showing AF, or refusing to heat is worth clearing the same day.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 PLATFORM (differentiator) -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · Amana is the Whirlpool value line</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Same platform, value pricing — good news for repairs.</h2>

    <p>Amana is Whirlpool's value-priced brand, and Amana dryers are built on the same Whirlpool platform with the same heating architecture: heating element, thermal fuse, thermal cut-off and high-limit thermostat, and on gas the same igniter, gas-valve coils, and flame sensor. Many <strong>Whirlpool</strong>, <strong>Maytag</strong>, and Whirlpool-built <strong>Kenmore</strong> dryers use the exact same parts.</p>

    <p>For a no-heat repair that's genuinely good news: the parts are mainstream, inexpensive, and the ones we stock for the whole family, so an Amana no-heat fix is usually quick and among the most affordable dryer repairs. The full platform breakdown lives on the <a href="/services/dryer-repair/whirlpool-not-heating/">Whirlpool not-heating</a> guide and applies to your Amana; the same-platform siblings are <a href="/services/dryer-repair/maytag-not-heating/">Maytag</a> and <a href="/services/dryer-repair/kenmore-not-heating/">Kenmore</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why an Amana dryer stops heating</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs find them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Blown thermal fuse from a restricted vent (most common)</h3>
      <p>A lint-clogged filter or duct chokes airflow, the dryer overheats, and the one-time thermal fuse trips and cuts the heat. It's replaced, not reset, and only stays fixed once the vent that blew it is cleared.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Heating element (electric models)</h3>
      <p>The element heats the air; when it burns open, the dryer tumbles cold. Tested for continuity and replaced inside the cabinet — a tech job.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Thermal cut-off / high-limit thermostat · AF airflow</h3>
      <p>The cut-off and high-limit protect against overheating and are usually replaced with the fuse as a kit. An AF message flags the same restricted airflow — clean the vent and clear it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Gas models — igniter, valve coils, flame sensor</h3>
      <p>On a gas Amana, no-heat usually means the burner isn't lighting: a weak igniter, failed gas-valve solenoid coils, or a bad flame sensor. An igniter that glows then goes out without lighting points to the coils. Gas parts are a tech job — never DIY the gas valve.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Clear the vent, reset the breaker.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Clean the lint filter and the full vent.</strong> Wash the filter, clear the hose behind the dryer, and confirm the exterior flap opens. This fixes most no-heat, AF, and blown-fuse calls — and removes the fire hazard.</li>
      <li><strong>Reset both breaker legs (electric).</strong> Flip the 240V double breaker fully off and on. A single tripped leg tumbles without heat.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm gas supply (gas).</strong> Verify the gas is on and other gas appliances work. Don't touch the gas valve or line.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear an AF message.</strong> After cleaning the vent, touch any key or power-cycle to dismiss it.</li>
      <li><strong>Watch whether it returns.</strong> Heat restored after a vent cleaning = it was airflow. Still cold on a clear vent = a failed part.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> vent cleaning and the breaker check are safe and fix most Amana no-heat calls. Do not open the cabinet to test the thermal fuse, element, or thermostat, and never disturb the gas line or valve on a gas dryer. A no-heat that survives a full vent cleaning is the handoff point.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When it's a tech job</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Clean vent, still cold? Time for a diagnostic.</h2>

    <p>When the vent is verified clear and the Amana still won't heat, the fault is inside: an open thermal fuse and cut-off (plus the vent that blew them), a burned-out heating element, or on gas the igniter, valve coils, or flame sensor. Our techs verify the vent first, then test the heating circuit and replace the failed parts — usually the fuse and cut-off as a kit. Because these are mainstream, affordable parts, a no-heat Amana is well worth fixing. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/dryer-repair/not-heating/">dryer not heating</a> guide (all brands), the <a href="/services/dryer-repair/error-codes/">dryer error codes</a> reference, and the platform anchor <a href="/services/dryer-repair/whirlpool-not-heating/">Whirlpool not heating</a>. A chronically clogged vent deserves a dedicated <a href="/services/dryer-vent-repair/">dryer vent</a> cleaning. Brand coverage: <a href="/brands/amana/">Amana appliance repair</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/dryer-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/rancho-cucamonga/dryer-repair/">Rancho Cucamonga</a>, and <a href="/temecula/dryer-repair/">Temecula</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Amana dryer not heating: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Amana dryer still cold after cleaning the vent? Call today.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

